{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>An Ammonite shall not enter… because…</b> It is difficult that it says <b>who did not meet</b>, for have we not found that the verse says (Deuteronomy 2:29) ״כַּאֲשֶׁר עָשׂוּ לִי בְּנֵי עֵשָׂו וגו׳ וְהַמּוֹאָבִים״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory explained (Rashi ad loc.) that it refers back to the statement ״אֹכֶל בַּכֶּסֶף״. Second, after they hired Balaam against them to curse them, what room is there to be concerned that they did not meet them with bread? Perhaps it is that He said <b>because of</b> etc. to indicate that for this reason alone the decree applies to distance them so that they shall not enter the assembly. And the meaning of ״קִדְּמוּ״ is to bring forth a gift to sustain their hearts with bread etc., for they were obligated to do so more than all the other nations, because of all the kindnesses that Abraham our father peace be upon him had done for them — for even their selling them bread and water was for Moab's own benefit, and also to lure them so as to bring them down in their cities. And this is what the verse precisely says <b>they did not meet you</b> — meaning, for your benefit." "<b>Further,</b> the verses may be explained according to their words of blessed memory in Yevamot 76a, where they said: From where do we derive these things, that a Moabite woman is permitted? Rabbi Yochanan said etc., for the verse states etc. Avner said to him: But we have learned ״מוֹאָבִי״ and not a Moabite woman. Doeg said to him: If so, then ״מַמְזֵר״ etc. — ״מִצְרִי״ and not an Egyptian woman? There it is different, for He specifies the reason ״עַל דְּבַר״ etc.: it is the way of a man etc., and it is not the way of a woman etc.; they should have come out to meet them etc., and women to meet women. They sought to proclaim this about him; immediately ״וַעֲמָשָׂא״ etc., which teaches that he girded his sword etc., and he said: Anyone who does not accept this halakhah shall be stabbed with the sword — I have it from the mouth of the court of Shmuel: ״מוֹאָבִי״ and not a Moabite woman. Nevertheless it is difficult, for Tamar translated it: ״כָּל כְּבוּדָּה בַת מֶלֶךְ פְּנִימָה.״ It was taught: ״עַמּוֹנִי״ and not an Ammonite woman, ״מוֹאָבִי״ and not a Moabite woman — the words of Rabbi Yehudah. Rabbi Shimon says: ״עַל דְּבַר אֲשֶׁר לֹא״ etc.; it is the way of a man to come out to meet etc. — this is their language. And the Tosafot wrote in the passage beginning ״כְּתַנָּאֵי״ and this is their language: And if you will say, according to Rabbi Yehudah the case of מִצְרִי is difficult, and our Rabbi Yitzchak says that in the case of Ammonite and Moabite He should have abbreviated and written Ammon and Moab, therefore we must interpret that it nearly excludes Ammonite woman and Moabite woman; but Egyptian — had He written מִצְרַיִם it would have been longer, and the reason He wrote אֲדוֹמִי and did not write אֱדוֹם is that alongside מִצְרִי He wrote אֲדוֹמִי — this is its language." "<b>But</b> the words of the Tosafot do not appear correct, for we have not found that the verse speaks a language incidentally alongside what it is going to say afterward — and how much more so in a place where there is room to err in halakhot. Further, if we suppose that the exclusion of מִצְרִי itself required the verse to write ״מִצְרִי,״ there would be room to say that the verse changed in what precedes it to speak in a form corresponding to it; but if מִצְרִי itself was not spoken by the verse as it should have been — for it should have written מִצְרַיִם to include an Egyptian woman, except that we say He did not wish to lengthen — how can we say that He wrote אֲדוֹמִי alongside מִצְרִי when מִצְרִי itself is what I am examining?" "<b>And to come</b> to the depth of the matter, it must be noted regarding the words of Avner: why did he grasp the rope at both ends? First he stated the inference ״מוֹאָבִי״ according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, and then went back to give a reason ״עַל דְּבַר אֲשֶׁר לֹא קִדְּמוּ״ etc. according to the opinion of Rabbi Shimon — for from the implication of the language it is proven that when he stated the second reason he did not retract the first reason. And logic supports this, for if not, why did Avner state a reason whose refutation is right at its side? Granted, according to Rabbi Yehudah in the view of the Tosafot — although we raised objections against them, at least according to their reasoning he said his words for a reason stated; but Avner — he revealed his mind in that he retracted from the difficulty of מִצְרִי, for he does not hold of distinguishing as the Tosafot distinguished." "<b>However,</b> it appears that Avner's intent follows the opinion of Rabbi Yochanan — who derives both from the statement ״מוֹאָבִי״ and from the statement ״עַל דְּבַר אֲשֶׁר״ etc.; for without ״עַל דְּבַר״ the derivation could be refuted from מִצְרִי. And the reason ״עַל דְּבַר אֲשֶׁר״ etc. alone — had He said ״מוֹאָב וְעַמּוֹן,״ even though He says ״עַל דְּבַר״ etc., we would have said: although the reason applies only to the men, the decree falls upon all of them alike. Further, the women too also come from disqualified seed, namely the males who are forbidden, and in such a case we follow only the father, as it is written (Numbers 1:2) ״לְמִשְׁפְּחֹתָם לְבֵית אֲבֹתָם.״ And if He had said ״עַמּוֹנִי וּמוֹאָבִי״ and we had no reason ״עַל דְּבַר,״ we would have said that He said ״מוֹאָבִי״ just as He said ״מִצְרִי״ and ״אֲדוֹמִי,״ and from both of them He derives: and not an Ammonite woman and not a Moabite woman." "<b>And therefore</b> Abner cited the two reasons that are mentioned, and the dispute between Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Shimon is along these lines: for Rabbi Yehudah maintains that the phrase ״מוֹאָבִי״ alone obligates saying וְלֹא מוֹאָבִית, and the phrase ״עַל דְּבַר״ obligates me neither to include nor to exclude, as I shall explain the reason; and the phrase ״מִצְרִי״ and ״אֲדוֹמִי״ — because the reason that the verse gives obligates me to include also Mitzrit and Edomit, since in all this the men are the same as the women, and also the sojourning in the land in which we were was equal in it for men and women, and the verse did not bother to say Mitzri and Edomit since there is no room to err as mentioned; and he did not hold like Rabbi Shimon to prove from the phrase ״עַל דְּבַר,״ either for the reason that we wrote in explaining Abner's words or for the reason that there is grounds to be concerned about the rejection of Doeg." "<b>And as for</b> what the Gemara interpreted ״כָּל כְּבוּדָּה״ (Psalms 45:14), there is room to suggest and say that perhaps it did not state ״כָּל כְּבוּדָּה בַת מֶלֶךְ פְּנִימָה״ except specifically regarding honor — not regarding a harlot — since we see that their deed was an act of brazenness, honor departed from them and the judgment reverted to say that they should have preceded, etc., and women to women. And there is reason to say that one should not bring proof from their abandonment to unchastity for two reasons: first, they were coerced because the men abandoned them; second, the Torah does not hold it against them for not doing something that is not the way of the world for women to do, on account of ״כָּל כְּבוּדָּה״ — even though they themselves degraded themselves in a different matter. From this the matter stands balanced and there is no proof from it, and He proved from the inference of Ammoni and Moavi." "<b>And Rabbi</b> Shimon maintains, as a ruling, that in any case they have the claim of ״כָּל כְּבוּדָּה״ (Psalms 45:14) and are exempt from the offering of bread, and we derive the verse's reason. And along this line their statement in the Gemara ״כְּתַנָּאֵי״ is explained — that it goes back to the saying ״כָּל כְּבוּדָּה בַת מֶלֶךְ פְּנִימָה,״ and therefore it attached it to it, for according to our explanation the entire core of the dispute between Rabbi Yehudah and Rabbi Shimon is only whether the claim of ״כָּל כְּבוּדָּה״ is applied to them or not." "<b>You thus</b> find three lines of reasoning in this Aggadah: according to the tradition of Rabbi Yochanan, who derives from the phrase ״מוֹאָבִי״ and the phrase ״עַל דְּבַר״; and according to the reasoning of Rabbi Yehudah, from the phrase ״עַמּוֹנִי״ alone; and according to the reasoning of Rabbi Shimon, the core of the derivation is from the phrase ״עַל דְּבַר.״" "<b>And now,</b> well according to the words of Rabbi Shimon, and also according to his tradition of Rabbi Yochanan there is a sound reason for the phrase <b>עַל דְּבַר</b>; but according to the reasoning of Rabbi Yehudah, who derives only from the word עַמּוֹנִי וּמוֹאָבִי, our question stands in its place: why was the verse required to say <b>עַל דְּבַר</b>? And it will be resolved as we wrote at the beginning of the matter." "<b>Once again</b> I have examined the inner meaning of this matter, and I see that there is no claim against the females for failing to come forth to meet them [with bread and water], for consider: even if they wanted in their hearts to come forth, would they be able to do so without their husbands' permission? And all the more so since their husbands were hostile toward them — they are coerced even if they did not come forth to meet them. From this it follows that the claim of ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא קִדְּמוּ״ applies only to the males, and the law stands: ״עַמּוֹנִי״ — and not Ammonitess; ״מוֹאָבִי״ — and not Moabitess. And Doeg's objection is an objector's [heretic's] objection, as Ahijah the Shilonite said when this difficulty was posed before him — he refuted it and said ״מִינָאָה״ [it is from him], as stated in the Midrash (Midrash Samuel 22)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {10 "The word מִינָאָה is left transliterated as the Or HaChaim presents it; it appears to be a condensed or elliptical expression used by Ahijah the Shilonite as cited in Midrash Samuel."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:28.553938658Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}